    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically it's all about identifying those specific failure points before they hit prod. what would you say is the most effective way to handle those \"stealthy\" issues that somehow bypass the initial testing gates? like things that don't trigger a standard error but still cause issues later?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Stealthy issues, like subtle race conditions or performance degradation under specific, rare load patterns, are the hardest to catch. To guard against these, you need to move beyond just unit tests and incorporate chaos engineering principles, or at least very robust, high-volume stress testing in staging. It's also vital to implement comprehensive observability—logging, tracing, and monitoring—so that when a sneaky bug *does* slip through, you can see the exact moment and nature of the failure in production. This proactive monitoring acts as a final, real-time safety net.",
      "source": "target",
